t to be able to see all  around the car a third is spaciousness  she needs room for at least five people  the fourth is fuel economy she covers  enough kilometers per day that she  really couldn't afford a car that  guzzles fuel feels locked the fifth is a  powerful engine she doesn't need a race  car but it should have enough horsepower  to get around and she says she knows of  a car that meets all these criteria  the masta - as it's called in Germany is  a snappy little car apparently made for  city dwellers and drivers and it's  snappy for a good reason it's one and a  half litre diesel gives its super mini  220 Newton meters of torque and against  a top score for fuel economy rated at  just 3.4 litres per hundred kilometers  on average with the exclusive line  package and options like LED headlights  the car we tested lists for about 21  thousand euros  [Music]  so as far as the feelgood factor goes  Michelle felt at home the moment she got  behind the wheel maybe because she  drives a Mazda to herself all the  controls are within easy reach so she  knew where everything was right away the  six gears let you cruise along  comfortably at high speeds and low revs  rare for a supermini compact  the next factor is visibility Michelle  is going to look for a parking spot and  find out how good it really is the rear  parking sensors make it easier to fit  into tight spots but they're only  available at extra cost the 650 euro  Touring package includes rain sensing  wipers a light sensor and heated drivers  and passenger seats  [Music]  Michelle finds the parking sensors very  helpful without them she wouldn't have a  good overview of the rear because of the  wide seat pillars  [Music]  but a relatively short length under 4  meters also helps when looking for  parking spots  [Music]  monster describes their supermini as  small but big on fun  [Music]  Mazda's range of engines delivered  greater performance for lower fuel  consumption thanks to the SKYACTIV  technology 