 diesel engine did very well on  third final criterion is the powerful  engine she doesn't expect a sports car  fuel consumption is more important for  everyday driving than a fast engine so  she recommends the car without  reservation and gives it good marks down  the list as far as she's concerned the  Mazda 2 is a good car for everyday use  and meets all her criteria and looks  nice besides  [Music]  armies of tradesmen have spent a good  part of their lives in the driver's seat  of the very first generation of the VW  bus called simply the t1 driver  Christoph Bowers taking one of the more  than 1.8 million t1s built out for a  spin the VW bus was the right vehicle at  the right time  no insanity Christophe recalls its  history starting in 1947 as West Germany  was still rebuilding from the Second  World War  back then trades people urgently needed  a practical utility van to carry their  tools and materials from A to B despite  the massive damage at the VW planet  Wolfsburg a solution was eventually  found at the time VW workers were  carrying parts and materials with a  transporter they'd built themselves by  putting a simple flatbed on a beetle  chassis and engine the parts carrier  inspired visiting vege VW importer Ben  pond to sketch what would become the VW  bus the engineering was based entirely  on the still new beetle the only VW in  production at the time Christoph  continues in the autumn of 1948 vew  chief Heinrich Nordhoff approved the  construction of Eve Tillett Eve and  designed to carry a 750 kilogram payload  based on the dependable VW Beetle but it  was not that easy nor cheap as it  happened the Beetle chassis tended to  break up under a full load on the poor  quality roads of the time the beetles  platform chassis wasn't strong enough to  handle the one and a half tonnes of a  fully loaded transporter so VW's  engineers created a new ladder chassis  with a single unit body but the axles  engine and many other parts were still  taken from the beetle it was 1950 and  all they needed was the right name  [Music]  by the conjecture stuff explains them in  Germany the VW bus is known as the bully  a mixture of bus and leaf of Ivan or  delivery van unfortunately the name was  already trademarked and other  suggestions such as Felix you live  bollocks and triumphant or were also  shot down by the Patent Office so what  the VW bus was given the simple  designation type - but it's German fans  didn't care they still know it as the  bully today the VW bus was a hit with a  car buying public in Germany and the  world over in 1954 the 100,000th unit  came off the production line  extraditions like the Samba bus and the  practical flatbed pickup soon appeared  the highly versatile VW bus enjoyed huge  popularity Christoph this is one of the  friendliest most likeable faces in  automotive history these funny bug guys  attract lots of nostalgic looks on the  road but the bus came close to looking  quite different production would have  been a great deal cheaper for a body  with square contours but tests in the  wind tunnel showed that round edges made  for much less wind resistance so the VW  bus was in fact one of the pioneers of  aerodynamics  a split front windshield the v-shape  front panel and a gargantuan VW emblem  gave the first generation of the bus its  now iconic face a higher ground  clearance and a practical flatbed with  an optional canvas cover help make the  t1 picked up the ideal construction site  workhorse the double cab was introduced  in 1958 with space for six workers and  their tools but heavy loads still push  the engine to its limit in praise of the  legendary 1.1 litre 25 horsepower boxer  engine taken from the beetle it's no  surprise that a load of one and a half  tons had the engine puffing and groaning  so the engineers gave it a shorter gear  ratio that brought the top speed down to  80 km/h but made it more dependable as a  workhorse should be the 1.2 liter engine  in this one delivers 34 horsepower  these days the village t1 buses are  mostly retired from their labors with no  load they can be surprisingly nippy even  if the maximum speed of just 95 km/h and  the partially synchronized transmission  will keep a driver in the country very  leisurely Christoph attributes the VW  buses enormous success to its incredible  versatility the version he's driving now  is a double cab pickup or as its fans  call it  Doka and dokas were the preferred  vehicle of builders the high load sil  helps it get through rough terrain such  as foundation pits with ease down  physically powerful behind fun hold up  the house  like all the other additions the double  cab pickup was a best-seller in 1958  Volkswagen moved production from  Wolfsburg to a plant built especially  for commercial vehicles in Hannover  three years later the one millionth VW  bus rolled off the assembly line  Kristof sums up the VW bus is the  ultimate multi-purpose vehicle a  workhorse a home an ambulance lifestyle  mobile the only limits are the owners  imagination the aerodynamically  optimized body and lightweight  construction left the competition back  for dust it became one of the driving  forces of West Germany's post-war  recovery a technology innovator and  without a doubt a milestone and  automotive history back then and today  the dhoka is the cheapest way to go if  you want to drive a first generation VW  t1 the t1 buses are still treasured  collectors items as indicated by their  sharp rise in prices in recent years  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  you

